course: dictionary
lesson: 1248
title: A heavy load
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A burden is a heavy load that you carry. It can be something in your arms, or a worry in your mind. «I hate being a burden to my friends» means the speaker feels they make things harder for their friends.
explain: Burden can also be a verb, so you can burden someone or be burdened by something. Test your meaning by swapping in load or weight. «A new school rule that burdens everyone else» means the rule gives everyone else a heavy load to carry.
explain: Clues help you work out burden. Words like heavy, carry and hard point towards it. «A weight off my shoulders» means a burden has been lifted, so you feel relieved.
fact: A burden is a heavy load that you carry.
fact: A burden can also be a difficult duty, worry or problem that is hard to deal with.
fact: As a verb, burden means to give someone a heavy load or a worry.
fact: «Ease the burden» means to make a heavy load lighter.
fact: Burden of proof is a law term: the duty to show that something is true.
exercise: «I hate being a burden to my friends.» What does burden mean here? | A kind of cheap bread | * A heavy load that makes things harder for them | A happy game they play together | A present wrapped in paper
exercise: «Carry each other's burdens» is a Bible verse about helping others. What does it tell us to do? | Carry bags for each other only at school | * Help each other with the heavy things in life | Count each other's money | Stop being friends when things get hard
exercise: Which phrase means that a worry has been lifted? | * A weight off my shoulders | Load the car | Heavy rain | Burden of proof
test:
«A new school rule that suits the prefects and burdens everyone else.» What does burdens mean here? | Lightens | Cheers up | * Gives a heavy load to | Tidies up
Which sentence uses burden as a verb? | The donkey's burden was heavy | * The worries burdened her all week | A burden of proof is needed | The load was carried up the hill
exam:
What is the best meaning of burden of proof? | A heavy bag | A kind of reward | * The duty to show that something is true | A long journey
